"""Arabic translation of *Final Fantasy III* (USA): the opening, to the airship.

A new game opens on the cliffs above Narshe: two Imperial soldiers and the
girl they lead in Magitek armour walk into the town, fight their way to the
frozen Esper, and the girl wakes in Arvis's house without her memory; the
guards come, she flees through the mines, and Locke and the Moogles get her
out (the first chapter, ``narshe``). Locke takes her to Figaro Castle, where
Edgar receives Kefka and the castle dives under the sand (``figaro``); they
reach South Figaro and Duncan's cabin (``south-figaro``), meet Sabin on Mt.
Kolts (``kolts``) and Banon at the Returners' hideout, and take the raft down
the Lete River to the scenario choice (``returners``). The third chapter is
the three scenarios and what follows: Locke and Celes in South Figaro
(``locke``), Banon's party at Narshe's checkpoint (``banon``), Sabin's road
(the Imperial camp and Shadow's dream, ``sabin``; Doma, Leo, Kefka's poison
and Cyan, ``doma``; the Phantom Train, ``train``; Baren Falls, the Veldt,
Gau, Mobliz and the Serpent Trench, ``veldt``; Nikeah and the return,
``nikeah``), Narshe's beginner's classroom (``classroom``), a few of the
ship's and the dining car's lines that sit among them (``ship``), and the
Elder, the battle for Narshe, Terra's flight and the town (``narshe-battle``).
The fourth chapter is the search for Terra: Figaro Castle again and the
brothers' memory (``castle``), Kohlingen and Rachel (``kohlingen``), Jidoor
(``jidoor``), Zozo, Ramuh's story and the plan (``zozo``), the Opera House
(``opera``) and Setzer's coin and airship (``airship``). The fifth chapter is
Vector under the Empire (``vector``), Banon's return to Narshe
(``narshe-return``), the Magitek Research Facility with Ifrit, Shiva, Cid,
Kefka and Celes's apparent betrayal (``facility``), the escape on the
airship (``escape``) and, back in Zozo, Terra's memory of the Esper world:
Maduin, Madonna and Gestahl's raid (``maduin``). The sixth chapter is the end of the
world of balance: Narshe and Banon's plan (``narshe-plan``), the sealed gate and
the Espers' rush (``gate``), Vector after them, its buried treasures and Kefka
in jail (``vector``), the Emperor's banquet, the soldiers and the rewards
(``banquet``), Cid and Setzer's memory of Daryl on the airship (``setzer``), and
the lines that sit among them: the towns of Maranda (``maranda``), Tzen
(``tzen``) and Albrook (``albrook``), Lone Wolf and Mog in Narshe with the
lessons and lines near them (``narshe-wolf``), and the world of ruin's dragons,
Duncan and Narshe's stone (``ruin``). The seventh chapter is the end of the world:
Albrook with Leo, Celes and Shadow, the voyage and Crescent Island (``voyage``),
Thamasa, Strago, Relm, the fire and the Mage Warriors (``thamasa``), Ultros at the
statues, the goddesses' story and Yura's Espers (``espers``), Kefka's betrayal,
Leo's death and the return (``leo``), and the Floating Continent, Shadow, the
Statues and the day the world changed (``continent``). The eighth chapter opens the
world of ruin: Celes and Cid on the island (``island``), Albrook and Tzen after the
fall with Sabin's collapsing house (``albrook-ruin``, ``tzen-ruin``), Strago and Relm
at the cult's tower (``cult``), Mobliz with Terra, Duane, Katarin and Phunbaba
(``mobliz-ruin``), Nikeah with the Crimson Robbers and Gerad (``nikeah-ruin``), South
Figaro after the fall (``south-figaro-ruin``), the cave to Figaro, Edgar unmasked and
the castle (``figaro-ruin``) and the Ancient Castle with Odin and the queen's diary
(``ancient-castle``). The ninth chapter gathers the friends: the Colosseum
(``colosseum``), Kohlingen and Setzer (``kohlingen-ruin``), Daryl's tomb with its
tombstone puzzle, adapted from reversed Latin letters to the order of four Arabic words
(``tomb``), Maranda's letters, Lola and Cyan (``maranda-ruin``), Gogo (``gogo``), the
Veldt cave with Relm, Shadow and Interceptor (``veldt-cave``) and Locke, the Phoenix and
Rachel's farewell (``phoenix``). The tenth chapter is Narshe with Tritoch, Mog and
Umaro (``narshe-ruin``), Jidoor's auction house with its bids (``auction``), the
Emperor's letter, Owzer's house, his diary, Relm and Chadarnook (``owzer``) and Cyan's
dream in Doma with the three stooges, his family and Wrexsoul (``dream``). The
translation
takes the messages of those scenes, and the fourteen characters' names the
game writes in them. Every other message stays in English.

Each original is pinned by its number (what the event script gives the
engine, and what the hooks match) and the SHA-256 of its bytes (its end
included), so the translation can be checked without the ROM and the build
refuses another text. Its command skeleton (``engines.ff6.command_skeleton``:
its commands with their bytes, but the layout) may be pinned too, so the
translation's commands are checked without the ROM as the build checks them
against the ROM's; the build's report carries every original's, for pinning.

The Arabic lives in ``classic_retro/translations/final-fantasy-iii.json``:
logical Unicode Arabic in the engine's notation (``engines.ff6_arabic``): a
line for each page, ``{line}`` where a line must end, ``{Terra}`` and the
other names for the characters' names, and the English's pauses and button
waits as tokens.

The messages are pinned from the ROM (``extract_originals``): numbers 0 (which
shares 1's text) to 63, the Narshe chapter; 64 to 368 but 364 and 365
(empty messages), the second chapter; 369 to 932 but 484 (empty), the
third; 933 to 1329, the fourth; 1330 to 1497, the fifth; 1498 to 1887 but 1536
(empty), the sixth; 1888 to 2167, the seventh; 2168 to 2418, the eighth; 2419 to 2613, the
ninth; and 2614 to 2799, the tenth.
"""

